3. The Argos language models
   One package for each direction of each language, from the index at
   https://github.com/argosopentech/argospm-index

   Every package carries its own README, which names the data it was
   trained on and the terms. A bundle has a copy of all of them in
   licences/argos-language-models.txt. In the 100 packages LibreTranslate
   1.9.6 installed on 17 November 2026:

   - 57 are OPUS-MT models repackaged for Argos Translate. Their READMEs
     say: "The original OPUS model from which this packaged model is
     derived is licensed CC-BY 4.0", and cite Jörg Tiedemann and Santhosh
     Thottingal, "OPUS-MT — Building open translation services for the
     World", Proceedings of the 22nd Annual Conference of the European
     Association for Machine Translation (EAMT), 2020.
   - Nearly all were trained on parallel text collected by OPUS
     (https://opus.nlpl.eu), which asks to be cited as: J. Tiedemann, 2012,
     "Parallel Data, Tools and Interfaces in OPUS", in Proceedings of the
     8th International Conference on Language Resources and Evaluation
     (LREC 2012).
   - The collections named include OpenSubtitles
     (http://www.opensubtitles.org/, which asks for this link), ParaCrawl
     (https://paracrawl.eu), CCMatrix, the United Nations Parallel Corpus,
     Europarl, TED talk transcripts, Tatoeba, Global Voices and WikiMatrix.
     Each has its own terms, given in the package's README.
   - Dictionary data in some packages is from Wiktionary, by way of
     Wiktextract (https://github.com/tatuylonen/wiktextract).
   - Some packages include pretrained Stanza models (see 2 above).

4. Ollama
   The app that runs TranslateGemma.

   Copyright (c) Ollama.
   MIT License.
   https://github.com/ollama/ollama

   A bundle carries Ollama's own signed release, unchanged, fetched from
   that address and checked against the checksum Ollama publishes.

5. TranslateGemma
   The translation model Ollama runs, built on Gemma 3.

   By Google. Described in: Google Translate Research Team and others,
   "TranslateGemma Technical Report", Google DeepMind, 2026.
   https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09012

   This is not open-source software. Its terms require this notice:

     Gemma is provided under and subject to the Gemma Terms of Use found at
     ai.google.dev/gemma/terms

   A bundle carries the model as Ollama publishes it, unchanged, and a copy
   of the terms that came with it, in the licences folder. If you pass a
   bundle to someone else, those terms require you to give them that copy
   and to tell them the model is subject to the use restrictions in its
   section 3.2. Those include the uses forbidden by the policy at
   ai.google.dev/gemma/prohibited_use_policy

   If a bundle was built with other models, their terms are in the licences
   folder in the same way.

6. Docker Desktop (Mac bundles only, unless built without it)
   What runs the LibreTranslate image.

   By Docker, Inc. Proprietary. Used under the Docker Subscription Service
   Agreement: https://www.docker.com/legal/docker-subscription-service-agreement/

   A bundle carries Docker's own signed installer for Apple Silicon Macs,
   unchanged, fetched from Docker and checked against Docker's checksum.

   Two things in that agreement, as last updated on 26 August 2026:

   - It is free to use in "a commercial undertaking with fewer than 250
     employees and less than US $10,000,000 (or equivalent local currency)
     in annual revenue". A larger organisation needs a paid subscription for
     every machine Docker Desktop is on. Docker's own summary of who may
     use it without paying:
     https://docs.docker.com/subscription/desktop-license/
   - It says the customer may not "distribute, license, sublicense, lease,
     rent, loan, or otherwise transfer the Services to any third party". So
     a bundle with Docker Desktop in it is for machines in your own
     organisation. For a bundle that is going to anyone else, build with
     --no-docker-desktop and have them fetch Docker Desktop from Docker.

7. The test files
   Extracts from the Wikipedia articles on Earth in five languages.

   By the contributors to those articles.
   Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International.
   https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

   test-files/README.md and test-files/sources.json give the revision each
   came from, a link to its contributors and what was changed. These files
   are in Hoshi's repository too, under this licence and not Hoshi's.

8. Reference evaluation data (repository only)
   The frozen FLORES-200 devtest subset in evaluation/corpus.json, released
   by the NLLB team at Meta/Facebook in 2022, and translations of its excerpts.
   Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International.
   https://github.com/facebookresearch/flores/tree/main/flores200
   https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

   evaluation/README.md records the source archive, selection method and
   changes; corpus.json records the original article URLs and row numbers.
   The publisher's licence text is in evaluation/LICENSE_CC-BY-SA.txt.
   These data are not part of the offline installation bundle.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
What Hoshi's own code runs on
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The web page is a Python program run inside a container made from the
LibreTranslate image, using libraries that image already holds:

  Flask            Pallets. BSD 3-Clause License.
                   https://github.com/pallets/flask
  Waitress         Zope Foundation and contributors. Zope Public License 2.1.
                   https://github.com/Pylons/waitress
  Requests         Kenneth Reitz and contributors. Apache License 2.0.
                   https://github.com/psf/requests
  lxml             The lxml contributors. BSD 3-Clause License.
                   https://github.com/lxml/lxml
  Beautiful Soup   Leonard Richardson. MIT License.
                   https://www.crummy.com/software/BeautifulSoup/
  PyMuPDF          Artifex Software, Inc. GNU Affero General Public License,
                   version 3, or a commercial licence from Artifex.
                   https://github.com/pymupdf/PyMuPDF

The page reads PDFs with PyMuPDF, which is one reason Hoshi is under the
GNU Affero General Public License.

The dropped-text check is written in Perl, which macOS and the LibreTranslate
image both include. On a Mac, the translate helper reads documents with
textutil, which is part of macOS.

The list of TranslateGemma's language codes in languages.tsv was taken from
https://ollama.com/library/translategemma on 16 November 2026.

The optional reference evaluation uses SacreBLEU, by Matt Post and
contributors, under the Apache License 2.0:
https://github.com/mjpost/sacrebleu
It is a development/evaluation dependency, not an installer dependency.
